      "rawToolArgs": "{\"comparisons\":[{\"requirementIndex\":0,\"lemmaName\":\"array_sum\",\"match\":true,\"discrepancy\":\"\",\"weakeningType\":\"none\",\"explanation\":\"Lemma correctly requires equal length and ensures pairwise sum in output array matching the requirement.\"},{\"requirementIndex\":1,\"lemmaName\":\"below_zero__ctrl_forall_to_not_exists\",\"match\":false,\"discrepancy\":\"The requirement says result should be true if balance falls below zero at any point (i.e., some s[i] < 0 for i from 1 to |operations|), but the lemma's postcondition for result==false only guarantees non-negative sums, and for result==true existence of negative sum - this matches on the surface. However the requirement specifies presum s[i] for i in 1..|operations| (not including s[0]), while the lemma's false case checks all i in 0..s.Length including s[0], which is trivially non-negative anyway so it's fine. Actually re-examining: the postcondition is logically equivalent to the requirement, this seems to match.\",\"weakeningType\":\"none\",\"explanation\":\"The lemma captures cumulative sum recurrence, and correctly ties result to whether any cumulative sum (excluding initial 0 which is always safe) is negative. This matches the requirement's intent.\"},{\"requirementIndex\":2,\"lemmaName\":\"binary_search__ctrl_contrapositive\",\"match\":false,\"discrepancy\":\"The requirement wants a strict boundary: array[index-1] < key <= array[index], meaning elements before n are strictly less than key AND elements from n onward are >= key (not just >=, but the key is at index n if present). The lemma's ensures clauses only guarantee elements before n are < key and elements at/after n are >= key, plus an extra clause that if not all elements are < key then n != a.Length. This extra clause is a weaker/redundant restatement, not required by original. However the core property (a[i]<key for i<n, a[i]>=key for i>=n) matches the original's intended binary search semantics (index-1 < key <= index). This seems to match.\",\"weakeningType\":\"none\",\"explanation\":\"The four...[truncated]"
    }
